course: biology
lesson: 401
title: The real animals of Wonderland: the dodo, the dormouse, the March hare, flamingos and hedgehogs
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland» is full of animals that talk, argue and play croquet, but most of them are real animals with real habits, and Lewis Carroll often chose them because of those habits or of old sayings about them.
explain: The Dodo who runs the Caucus-race was a real bird, a big flightless pigeon from the island of Mauritius, and it died out long before Alice was written: no one alive has seen a dodo.
explain: The Dormouse who keeps falling asleep at the tea-party is sleepy for a true reason: dormice really sleep through the whole winter, and in summer they sleep all day and wake at night.
explain: The March Hare is mad because of an old saying, «mad as a March hare»: in spring, hares chase each other and stand up and «box» in the fields. And the flamingos and hedgehogs used as croquet mallets and balls are real too, though no real bird or hedgehog would put up with that.
explain: So when you read, ask two questions: which parts are true about the animal, and which parts are the joke?
fact: The dodo was a large bird that could not fly, related to pigeons and doves, and it lived only on the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean.
fact: The dodo had no enemies on its island, so it was not afraid of people; sailors, and the rats, pigs and cats they brought, wiped it out, and the last dodos are thought to have died around 1680.
fact: Lewis Carroll's real name was Charles Dodgson, and many people think the Dodo in the book is a joke about himself, because he stammered and sometimes said «Do-do-Dodgson».
fact: The Lory at the Caucus-race is a real bird: a small, brightly coloured parrot from Australia, New Guinea and nearby islands that feeds on nectar with a brush-tipped tongue.
fact: The hazel dormouse is a small, gold-brown rodent with a fluffy tail that lives in woods and hedges in Britain and Europe; it is now rare in Britain.
fact: A hazel dormouse hibernates from about October to April, curled in a nest on the ground, and in summer it sleeps by day and is busy at night, so at an afternoon tea it would be fast asleep.
fact: Hares are not rodents; they are bigger than rabbits, with longer ears and legs, they live above ground instead of in burrows, and their babies are born with fur and open eyes.
fact: In spring hares chase each other and stand up on their back legs to «box»; this is usually a female hitting a male who is bothering her, not two males fighting.
fact: Flamingos are tall pink wading birds that feed with their heads upside down in shallow water, sieving tiny shrimps and algae with their beaks; their pink comes from that food.
fact: A real flamingo's long neck is soft and bends, which is exactly why Alice's flamingo keeps twisting round to look at her: it would be a useless croquet mallet.
fact: A hedgehog has about 5,000 to 7,000 spines and rolls into a tight prickly ball when frightened, which is why Carroll made hedgehogs into croquet balls that unroll and walk away.
fact: Hedgehogs eat beetles, worms and slugs; milk upsets their stomachs, so the right drink to leave out for one is a dish of water.
fact: Alice is called a «serpent» by the Pigeon because of her long neck; real pigeons eat seeds and grain, not eggs, but snakes do eat birds' eggs, so the Pigeon's fear is real for birds.
fact: Cats cannot grin, vanish or talk; «to grin like a Cheshire cat» was an old saying before Carroll, and nobody is quite sure where it came from.
exercise: Where did the dodo live? | Only on the island of Mauritius | All over the forests of Africa | In the cold seas around Antarctica | On the moors of northern England
exercise: Which birds were dodos related to? | Pigeons and doves | Penguins and puffins | Ducks and geese | Owls and hawks
exercise: Why was the dodo not afraid of the sailors? | It had never had any enemies on its island | It was much too big and heavy for the sailors to catch | It was too fast for anyone to chase | It could fly away whenever it liked
exercise: When did the last dodos probably die? | Around 1680 | Around 1865 | During the time of the dinosaurs | Just a few years ago
exercise: Whose name may be hidden in the book's Dodo? | The author's own, Charles Dodgson | The name of Alice's older sister, who reads by the river | The Queen of England's name | The name of Alice's cat Dinah
exercise: What is a real lory? | A small bright parrot that drinks nectar | A large grey bird that cannot fly | A kind of seagull from the English coast | A water bird with very long pink legs
exercise: What does a hazel dormouse do in winter? | It hibernates in a nest for months | It flies south to Africa with the swallows | It digs deep tunnels under the snow | It moves into people's houses to keep warm
exercise: When is a dormouse awake in summer? | At night | Only at noon | All day and all night | Only when it is raining
exercise: Which is true of hares? | They live above ground and are born with fur | They are rodents like mice and rats | They dig deep burrows under the fields just like rabbits do | They are smaller than most pet rabbits
exercise: What is a hare usually doing when it «boxes» in spring? | A female is pushing away a male who bothers her | Two old males are fighting each other to the death for the field | A mother is teaching her babies to swim | It is scratching off its thick winter fur
exercise: How does a flamingo feed? | It sieves tiny shrimps and algae, head upside down | It dives deep under the sea for big fish | It digs worms and beetles out of the wet grass with its long feet | It eats the roses in people's gardens
exercise: What makes a flamingo pink? | The pigment in the food it eats | It is painted by the zoo keepers | The pink water of the lake it stands in | Its blood showing through its feathers
exercise: What does a frightened hedgehog do? | It rolls into a tight prickly ball | It runs up the nearest tree | It lies on its back and plays dead with its tongue out | It sprays a very bad smell
exercise: What should you leave out for a hedgehog to drink? | A dish of water | A saucer of cow's milk | A cup of sweet tea | A bowl of orange juice
exercise: What do real pigeons eat? | Seeds and grain | Mostly birds' eggs | Small snakes | Only leaves of trees
exercise: Which part of the Cheshire Cat is impossible for a real cat? | Grinning and then vanishing, all but its grin | Having long claws and a great many sharp teeth, as Alice saw | Sitting on the branch of a tree | Purring when it is pleased
test: «dodos are still on some island somewhere, my cousin saw one» What is true? | Dodos are extinct: the last ones died around 1680, so nobody alive has seen one | Dodos still live in the mountains of Mauritius but they are very shy | Your cousin probably saw one in a zoo in London, where a few are still kept and bred | Dodos turned into the pigeons we see in town today
test: Why did a bird with no enemies die out so fast once people came? | It did not know to run, and people, rats, pigs and cats ate it and its eggs | It caught a cold from the sailors and every single one of them died of it within a year | The island sank into the sea soon after the sailors came | It flew away to another island and got lost on the way
test: At the Mad Tea-Party the Dormouse sleeps through nearly everything. Why does that suit a real dormouse? | Real dormice sleep by day and hibernate for months | Real dormice are always ill and need more rest | Real dormice only wake up when they hear music | Real dormice sleep standing on their heads
test: A girl in Devon sees two hares standing up and boxing in a March field. What is most likely happening? | A female is fending off a male who is chasing her | Two brothers are playing a game they learned as babies | Two hares are fighting over a carrot | A hare is boxing its own reflection in a puddle
test: «hares r just big rabbits innit, same thing» What is right? | No: hares are a different animal; they are bigger, live above ground and their babies have fur at birth | Yes: a hare is a rabbit that has grown very old | Yes: a hare is just a rabbit that has grown very big and very old, with longer ears from all that listening | No: hares are rodents and rabbits are not
test: In the book, Alice's flamingo keeps twisting its neck to look at her. What does that tell you about real flamingos? | Their long necks are soft and bendy, not stiff like a mallet | Their necks are made of one long solid bone and cannot bend at all, even to eat | They cannot see anything in front of them | They always look backwards when they walk
test: A cartoon shows a hedgehog curled up and rolled across a lawn like a ball. What would a real frightened hedgehog do once it felt safe again? | Unroll and walk off, just like the hedgehogs in Alice | Stay rolled up for the rest of its life | Explode its spines all over the lawn | Bounce like a rubber ball until it stops
test: «found a hedgehog in the garden, gave it a big bowl of milk» What would you say? | Give it water instead, milk upsets a hedgehog's stomach | Good, milk is its favourite food in the wild | Good idea, but add some sugar so it gets more energy for the night | Warm the milk first and it will be fine
test: The Pigeon screams that Alice is a serpent who eats eggs. Which part of its fear is true about real animals? | Snakes really do eat birds' eggs | Girls with long necks really do eat eggs | Pigeons really do eat snakes | Pigeons really do lay eggs on the ground in the open
test: Which animal from the book could you still see alive today? | A flamingo | A dodo | A Gryphon | A Mock Turtle
test: A Cheshire Cat's grin stays after the cat has gone. Why is this a joke and not science? | A grin is just the shape of a face, so it cannot exist without the face | Cats never show their teeth at all, so it is a lie | Real Cheshire cats can do this, but only at night | A real cat's grin can stay in the air for a few seconds after the cat has run off
test: ★★★ A park keeper counts the croquet things in a story: 4 players, each with one flamingo and one hedgehog, and 3 hedgehogs have walked away. How many animals are still at the game, not counting the players? | 5 | 8 | 4 | 3
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the dodo was exterminated within a short period of human contact. | It had evolved without predators and possessed no fear of humans or of the animals they introduced | It was hunted for its feathers, which were greatly prized for the decoration of ladies' hats in Paris and London | It was unable to survive the cold winters that followed the arrival of ships | It consumed a poisonous plant brought by the sailors and perished
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the winter behaviour of the hazel dormouse? | It hibernates in a nest for several months, living upon its stored fat | It migrates in large flocks to warmer lands across the sea | It remains fully active and gathers food beneath the snow | It moves into houses and barns in search of warmth
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning hares observed boxing in the spring. | The behaviour is most commonly a female repelling the advances of a male | The behaviour invariably signifies a contest between two adult males for possession of a territory | The behaviour is a means of removing the winter coat | The behaviour is confined to the month of March only
exam: Ascertain the drink which it is appropriate to provide for a wild hedgehog. | Water, since cow's milk disagrees with its digestion | Milk, since it constitutes its natural nourishment | Sweetened tea, to restore its energy | Fruit juice, to supply vitamins
